====================================================================================================
v103  TWIRL CALIBRATION UNDER NOISE:  does it survive, and how does it fail?
====================================================================================================
  N=3 crosstalk, M=9, T=0.25, shots=65536, 4 probes -> 24 circuits per estimate
  Noise: depolarising p2 on two-qubit gates, p2/10 on one-qubit, readout 2*p2.
  3 seeds per row. TIER A: real circuits, noisy simulator, finite shots.

====================================================================================================
PART 1  THE NOISE SWEEP, at reps=1 (the shallow device, licensed by v102)
====================================================================================================
  's' is the best global scale chat ~ s*c_true; s=1 is unattenuated.
  'cos' is direction only. 'raw' is mean rel err; 'shape' is mean rel err
  AFTER dividing s out - the part no rescaling can recover.

      p2        s        cos        raw err            shape err
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   0.0e+00   0.9831   0.99917   0.0682 +- 0.0125    0.0630 +- 0.0070
   1.0e-04   0.9809   0.99906   0.0769 +- 0.0053    0.0748 +- 0.0039
   3.0e-04   0.9736   0.99897   0.0873 +- 0.0159    0.0830 +- 0.0205
   1.0e-03   0.9352   0.99940   0.0896 +- 0.0161    0.0626 +- 0.0275
   3.0e-03   0.8589   0.99929   0.1485 +- 0.0065    0.0840 +- 0.0226
   1.0e-02   0.6301   0.99464   0.3844 +- 0.0265    0.1392 +- 0.0232

====================================================================================================
PART 2  THE DEPTH PENALTY:  reps=1 against reps=12 under the same noise
====================================================================================================
  v102: noiselessly reps is free (spread 0.0010 vs seed sd 0.0100), and the
  device's own Trotter error is 2.0e-03 at reps=1, far under the estimator's.
  Depth goes 70 -> 840. Under noise the extra reps should be pure cost.

      p2      reps      s        cos       raw err
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------
   1.0e-04      1    0.9809   0.99906   0.0769 +- 0.0053
   1.0e-04     12    0.9606   0.99943   0.0725 +- 0.0188

   1.0e-03      1    0.9352   0.99940   0.0896 +- 0.0161
   1.0e-03     12    0.7517   0.99753   0.3026 +- 0.0129

   3.0e-03      1    0.8589   0.99929   0.1485 +- 0.0065
   3.0e-03     12    0.4586   0.98024   0.5126 +- 0.0542

====================================================================================================
READING IT
====================================================================================================
  noiseless     s=0.9831  cos=0.99917  raw=0.0682  shape=0.0630
  p2=1e-02     s=0.6301  cos=0.99464  raw=0.3844  shape=0.1392

  Across the whole sweep the cosine moves 0.00452 and the shape error moves
  0.0762, while the scale s moves 0.3530.

  PREDICTION HELD. Noise costs SCALE, not SHAPE: the direction of the
  estimate survives while its magnitude contracts. That is the
  RECOVERABLE-IN-PRINCIPLE failure mode, and it is the good outcome - but
  note precisely what is and is not shown here.

  SHOWN: the information is not destroyed. cos stays above 0.994 across
  four orders of magnitude of p2, and at p2=1e-3 the shape error is
  statistically identical to the noiseless one.

  NOT SHOWN: that s can be recovered in practice. Dividing it out here
  uses c_true, which a real calibration does not have. Recovering s needs
  its own protocol - one independently known coefficient, or an RB-style
  fidelity measurement on the same circuit structure - and NONE of that is
  measured in this file. Treat 'correctable' as a hypothesis with a clear
  target, not as a result.

  A BONUS, and a hint that the same correction serves twice: s = 0.9831
  ALREADY sits below 1 with no noise at all. That is the first-order
  truncation bias in T - the estimator systematically under-reads, exactly
  as twirl_cal reports - showing up as a scale factor too. One measured s
  would remove both the truncation bias and the noise attenuation.

  Scope: one coefficient draw, N=3, hand-built depolarising model, no T1/T2
  idle decay during the device evolution. The LEVELS are illustrative; the
  TREND and the scale/shape split are the result.
